 "drivingQuestion": "What is the source for not opening an entrance opposite an entrance?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "source given",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_batra.60a.5",
   "daf": "60a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן, דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וַיִּשָּׂא בִלְעָם אֶת עֵינָיו, וַיַּרְא אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל שֹׁכֵן לִשְׁבָטָיו״ – מָה רָאָה? רָאָה שֶׁאֵין פִּתְחֵי אׇהֳלֵיהֶם מְכֻוּוֹנִין זֶה לְזֶה, אָמַר: רְאוּיִן הַלָּלוּ שֶׁתִּשְׁרֶה עֲלֵיהֶם שְׁכִינָה.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The Gemara asks: «From where are these matters,» i.e., that one may not open an entrance opposite another entrance, or a window opposite another window, derived? «Rabbi Yoḥanan says that» the «verse states: “And Balaam lifted up his eyes, and he saw Israel dwelling tribe by tribe;» and the spirit of God came upon him” (Numbers 24:2). The Gemara explains: «What» was it that Balaam «saw» that so inspired him? He «saw that the entrances of their tents were not aligned with each other,» ensuring that each family enjoyed a measure of privacy. And he «said:» If this is the case, «these» people «are worthy of having the Divine Presence rest on them.»"
  }
 ],
